<h2>1. Prepare</h2>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/ShouryaSengar/jira-bug-dashboard.git
cd jira-bug-dashboard
npm install                       # root: TypeScript for the shared frontend
cd cloud && npm install           # Cloud: wrangler + workers types (kept separate from the Local edition)</code></pre>
<p>Optional but recommended: run it locally against the mock first (no accounts needed): see <a href="testing.html">How this app is tested</a>.</p>

<h2>2. Log Wrangler in to Cloudflare</h2>
<pre><code>npx wrangler login                 # or: npx wrangler login --browser=false
npx wrangler whoami                # shows your account name and id</code></pre>
<div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-title">What went wrong for us</span>The first link opened in the default browser (Edge) while we approved in another (Brave): Cloudflare answered "No CSRF value available in the session cookie". A stale tab then sent an old <code>state</code> ("query string parameter doesn't match"). Fix: <code>--browser=false</code>, open the printed link in the browser you are signed in with, approve once, close old tabs.</div>

<h2>3. Create the database</h2>
<pre><code>npx wrangler d1 create jbd-cloud</code></pre>
<p>Copy the printed <code>database_id</code> into <code>cloud/wrangler.jsonc</code> under <code>env.production.d1_databases</code>, keeping <code>"binding": "DB"</code>. (Wrangler offers to add it itself with a different binding name; decline.) Then create the tables:</p>
<pre><code>npm run build                                                   # shared frontend + dist-assets
npx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --remote --env production   # 0001, 0002…</code></pre>

<h2>4. First deploy, to get your URL</h2>
<pre><code>npx wrangler deploy --env production</code></pre>
<div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-title">workers.dev subdomain</span>A new account must have a <code>workers.dev</code> subdomain. Wrangler asks interactively; in a non-interactive shell it fails and prints a dashboard link (which for us was a 404, the dashboard had moved). Check whether you already have one: <code>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/&lt;account-id&gt;/workers/subdomain</code>, or in the dashboard under Workers &amp; Pages. Ours already existed (<code>shouryacode2021</code>).</div>
<p>Your URL is <code>https://jira-bug-dashboard-cloud.&lt;subdomain&gt;.workers.dev</code>. Put it in <code>env.production.vars.APP_ORIGIN</code>. Until configuration is complete, <code>/api/config</code> answers <em>503 "not configured: missing …"</em> and the page falls back to demo mode. That is intended.</p>

<h2>5. Keys and secrets</h2>
<pre><code>node scripts/set-prod-secrets.mjs mailto:you@example.com</code></pre>
<p>This one script generates a VAPID key pair and a 32-byte token-encryption key in memory, pipes <code>TOKEN_ENC_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code> and <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code> straight into <code>wrangler secret put</code>, and writes only the public key into <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>. Check with <code>npx wrangler secret list --env production</code> (names only). Redeploy afterwards.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-title">Run it once</span>Running it again replaces the keys: existing push subscriptions stop working and every user must sign in again.</div>

<h2>6. Register the Atlassian app</h2>
<p>At <a href="https://developer.atlassian.com/console/myapps/">developer.atlassian.com/console/myapps</a>: <em>Create → OAuth 2.0 integration</em>; Permissions: Jira API classic scopes <code>read:jira-work</code>, <code>read:jira-user</code>, and User identity API; Authorization: callback <code>https://&lt;your-url&gt;/auth/callback</code>, resource-level. Full details: <a href="atlassian-console.html">The developer console</a>.</p>

<h2>7. Client id and secret</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Client ID</strong> (public): into <code>env.production.vars.ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Secret</strong>: type it yourself, it never goes into a file:
<pre><code>npx wrangler secret put ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET --env production</code></pre></li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Deploy and sign in</h2>
<pre><code>npx wrangler deploy --env production
curl https://&lt;your-url&gt;/api/config          # {"edition":"cloud","configured":false,"signedIn":false,…}
npx wrangler tail --env production --format pretty   # watch while you sign in</code></pre>
<p>Open the URL, <em>Sign in with Atlassian</em>, choose your site, Accept. You should land on For You. In the tail you will see <code>/auth/login → /auth/callback → /api/mine</code>, and within five minutes a cron line with <code>"checked": 1</code> (your baseline). Verify at rest (read-only):</p>
<pre><code>npx wrangler d1 execute DB --remote --env production --command \
  "SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users) users, (SELECT status FROM oauth_grants) grant_status"</code></pre>

<h2>9. Share with others</h2>
<p>Set the app's <strong>Distribution</strong> to <em>Sharing within organization</em> or <em>Sharing with anyone</em>, add your privacy policy (<code>https://&lt;your-url&gt;/privacy</code>), declare that personal data is stored (the Personal Data Reporting API is implemented), and send colleagues the URL. Company admins may need to approve third-party apps.</p>

<h2>Later deploys</h2>
<pre><code>cd cloud
npm run deploy        # = build + remote migrations + wrangler deploy</code></pre>
<p>Adding a new migration file? It runs automatically on the next <code>npm run deploy</code>. Want to check first? <code>npm run deploy:dry</code>.</p>

<h2>Checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>☐ <code>wrangler whoami</code> shows the right account</li>
  <li>☐ <code>database_id</code> set, migrations applied</li>
  <li>☐ <code>APP_ORIGIN</code>, <code>ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY</code> set (no <code>REPLACE_</code> left)</li>
  <li>☐ 4 secrets listed: <code>ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, <code>TOKEN_ENC_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code></li>
  <li>☐ Callback URL in Atlassian matches exactly</li>
  <li>☐ <code>/api/config</code> returns 200 with <code>"edition":"cloud"</code></li>
  <li>☐ Sign-in works; baseline appears in the tail</li>
  <li>☐ JARVIS → Enable notifications → Send a test push arrives</li>
  <li>☐ Privacy policy reachable; distribution set if sharing</li>
</ul>
